.TH LD86 1
.SH NAME
ld86 \- link editor for as86 object files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ld86
[\ \-03Mimrsty\ ] [\ \-o\ outfile\ ] [\ \-Llibdir\ ] [\ \-lx\ ] [\ \-Ccrtfile\ ]
[\ \-Olibfile\ ] [\ \-Ttextaddr\ ] [\ \-Ddataaddr\ ] [\ \-Hheaptop\ ] file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ld86
combines object files made by
.BR as86 (1)
into one program, joining their text and data and resolving the
references each makes to symbols the others define. A
.I file
that is a library made by
.BR ar86 (1)
contributes those of its members that define a symbol some file
before it on the command line needs and nothing has yet defined.
.PP
ld86 writes the a.out format of the as86 tools, not a XENIX executable;
.BR cc (1)
links programs with
.B "ld86 \-0 \-i \-y"
and then converts its output with /usr/lib/cc/aout2x. Use cc to make
programs, and ld86 alone only to look at a link (with
.B \-M
or
.BR \-m ).
.PP
The options are:
.TP 14
.BI \-o " outfile"
Write the program to
.I outfile
instead of a.out.
.TP
.B \-0
Make a 16-bit program for the 8086 (the default).
.TP
.B \-3
Make a 32-bit program for the 80386.
.TP
.B \-i
Give the program separate instruction and data spaces: text and data
each start at address 0, each up to 64K. cc uses it.
.TP
.B \-s
Leave out the symbol table.
.TP
.B \-y
Keep symbol names longer than 8 characters whole in the symbol table,
in extra records that follow each name. cc uses it, and aout2x joins
them again.
.TP
.B \-r
Make a relocatable object file, from a single object file, for further
linking.
.TP
.B \-M
Print the symbols linked, with their values, on the standard output.
.TP
.B \-m
Print the modules linked on the standard output.
.TP
.B \-t
Trace the modules as they are looked at.
.TP
.BI \-L libdir
Add
.I libdir
to the front of the directories searched for
.BR \-l ,
.B \-C
and
.BR \-O .
.TP
.BI \-l x
Link with the library
.BI lib x .a
from those directories.
.TP
.BI \-C x
Link with the object file
.BI crt x .o
from those directories, first.
.TP
.BI \-O x
Link with the file
.I x
from those directories.
.TP
.BI \-T textaddr
.PD 0
.TP
.BI \-D dataaddr
.PD
The addresses at which the text and the data start, in the C forms of
.BR strtoul (3):
decimal, 0x for hexadecimal, 0 for octal.
.TP
.BI \-H heaptop
The top of the heap, where the stack starts.
.PP
An option without an argument is turned off by a \- after its letter.
.PP
The options
.B \-d
(a file with no header),
.B \-N
and
.B \-z
(the formats of other systems) are not useful for XENIX.
.SS Defined symbols
ld86 defines __etext, __edata and __end, the addresses of the end of
the text, of the initialized data and of the uninitialized data; a C
program declares them as
.PP
.nf
	extern char _etext, _edata, _end;
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP 10
a.out
the output
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
"undefined symbol:
.IR name \(rq
for each symbol that is used but not defined; "warning:
.I name
redefined" for one defined in two files, when the first definition is
used. The exit status is 0 if the link succeeded.
.SH SEE ALSO
cc(1), as86(1), ar86(1), nm(1), size(1)
.SH HISTORY
An
.B ld
command first appeared in Version 1 AT&T UNIX.
.SH AUTHORS
ld86 was written by Bruce Evans.
.SH BUGS
ld86 reads every member of a library, so a library needs no index of
its symbols; but a member is taken only for a symbol needed before the
library on the command line, so a library must come after the files
that use it, and two libraries that use each other may need to be named
twice.
